{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KbiDBSm5YXFNf6xv2rjhqi","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CODATA lattice parameter of silicon 543.1020511 pm NIST\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2010\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.5425\"},{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787\"},{\"title\":\"xray 1998\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_1998.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2014\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2014.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2010\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2010.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2002\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2002.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2006\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2006.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/697111\"},{\"title\":\"Template:Physical constants\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst\"}]\n\nThe value 543.1020511 pm matches the current CODATA silicon lattice parameter, but I didn't find it on a NIST page. The only source that gives it is a Wikipedia template that tracks CODATA 2022. That template lists the lattice parameter as a = 5.431020511(89)×10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6×10⁻⁸. Its listed value is in metres, so 543.1020511 pm is the same number converted.\n\nOlder NIST X-ray tables give different values. The 2006 table lists the lattice parameter as 543.102 064(14)×10⁻¹² m, which is about 543.102064 pm and differs from the value you asked about.\n\nThe CODATA definition also matters. The lattice parameter refers to an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections, and the stated conditions are vacuum at 22.5 °C. The CODATA 2022 review says the adopted reference conditions for natural silicon are t₉₀ = 22.5 °C and p = 0 (vacuum); that snippet came from the 2010 review, so treat it as the long-standing convention rather than a 2022 quote.\n\nI could not confirm the value from the NIST CODATA 2022 page directly, so check nist.gov/pml/fundamental-physical-constants for the authoritative figure.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f59bab16-4a7f-44bd-99fb-e96e7fa3fef6","uuid":"5db9c6c5-ddc2-4f2e-b905-17163c8e1e5b","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:52:35.199Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CODATA lattice parameter of silicon 543.1020511 pm NIST","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0133ta9siabQfrmkYnsxQcFX","content":[{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2010","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.5425"},{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787"},{"title":"xray 1998","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_1998.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2014","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2014.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2010","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2010.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2002","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2002.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2006","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2006.pdf"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/697111"},{"title":"Template:Physical constants","url":"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst"}]},"The value 543.1020511 pm matches the current CODATA silicon lattice parameter, but I didn't find it on a NIST page. The only source that gives it is a Wikipedia template that tracks CODATA 2022. That template lists the lattice parameter as a = 5.431020511(89)×10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6×10⁻⁸. Its listed value is in metres, so 543.1020511 pm is the same number converted.\n\nOlder NIST X-ray tables give different values. The 2006 table lists the lattice parameter as 543.102 064(14)×10⁻¹² m, which is about 543.102064 pm and differs from the value you asked about.\n\nThe CODATA definition also matters. The lattice parameter refers to an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections, and the stated conditions are vacuum at 22.5 °C. The CODATA 2022 review says the adopted reference conditions for natural silicon are t₉₀ = 22.5 °C and p = 0 (vacuum); that snippet came from the 2010 review, so treat it as the long-standing convention rather than a 2022 quote.\n\nI could not confirm the value from the NIST CODATA 2022 page directly, so check nist.gov/pml/fundamental-physical-constants for the authoritative figure."],"durationSeconds":5.54484055600001,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KbiDBSm5YXFNf6xv2rjhqi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126Sz1TtLXcg7uD85jCLWzD","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Haas Tran Blaha 2009 Phys Rev B 79 085104 lattice constants PBE experiment Si Ge C Cu Al Ag table\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals\",\"url\":\"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529\"},{\"title\":\"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals\"},{\"title\":\"dx.doi.org\",\"url\":\"https://dx.doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.79.209902\"},{\"title\":\"Assessing the performance of recent density functionals for bulk solids\",\"url\":\"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/assessing-the-performance-of-recent-density-functionals-for-bulk-solids/811858929981063168-529\"},{\"title\":\"qbox.ucdavis.edu\",\"url\":\"https://qbox.ucdavis.edu/qbox-list/viewtopic.php?p=476\"},{\"title\":\"suppl mater NJP108118 R1\",\"url\":\"https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_2599505_6/component/file_2618826/suppl-mater-NJP108118-R1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Assessing the performance of recent density functionals for bulk solids\",\"url\":\"https://libmast.utm.my/Record/mit-1721.1%2F51869/Details\"},{\"title\":\"nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3331871\"},{\"title\":\"assessing the performance of recent density functionals for 45szrev607\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/assessing-the-performance-of-recent-density-functionals-for-45szrev607.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search found the paper and several secondary references, but not the table values you asked for (Si, Ge, C, Cu, Al, Ag).\n\n- **The paper:** Haas, Tran, and Blaha's \"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals\" appeared in Phys. Rev. B 79, 085104 (2009). It assesses LDA, GGA (PBE, AM05, WC, PBEsol, SOGGA), and TPSS for lattice constants and bulk moduli on a set of 60 cubic solids.\n- **Experimental reference:** The experimental lattice constants were corrected for zero-point anharmonic expansion (ZPAE).\n- **Elemental results:** A secondary summary reports that for Al, most functionals except PBE yield accurate lattice constants. That suggests PBE is the outlier for Al, but I could not confirm the numerical Al, Si, Ge, C, Cu, or Ag values from these results.\n- **Erratum:** A related DOI, 10.1103/PhysRevB.79.209902, appears in the results, which suggests a published erratum. I did not check what it changed, so verify any table values against the original paper and erratum.\n- **Other sources:** A QBox forum post cites the paper's PBE value for bcc Fe as 2.833 Å, which is the only specific PBE number I found. It is not one of your requested elements.\n\nTo get the table values, the next step is to open the PRB article directly (DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.79.085104) or a full-text copy.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f59bab16-4a7f-44bd-99fb-e96e7fa3fef6","uuid":"722915b8-50e6-4bbf-96dd-73a4ec3f1b44","timestamp":"2026-11-01T02:52:35.284Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Haas Tran Blaha 2009 Phys Rev B 79 085104 lattice constants PBE experiment Si Ge C Cu Al Ag table","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MXjHfJAUSGgb7L3dYLpYiB","content":[{"title":"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z","url":"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf"},{"title":"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals","url":"https://www.